WEBVTT

NOTE F6: large flow. Ward nurse Helen (main speaker) and doctor Ravi (alternative views). 33 steps, 5 actors, 2 error flows (one returns or continues, one can end in failure). About 8 minutes of dialogue: the length target for PE-06 (60 minutes) needs a real transcript. Synthetic.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
<v Interviewer>Thanks for making time. Can you take me through discharging a patient from the ward?</v>

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:18.000
<v Helen>Of course. What we're after is getting someone home safely, with the right medicines and support, and freeing the bed for the next person.</v>

3
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
<v Helen>I'm Helen, I'm a staff nurse on the ward. Ravi's one of our doctors, he'll jump in.</v>

4
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000
<v Ravi>Hi. Yeah, I'll chip in on the medical side.</v>

5
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
<v Interviewer>How often do you do a discharge?</v>

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
<v Helen>Every day. Several a shift, usually.</v>

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.000
<v Helen>It starts on the ward round. The consultant or Ravi decides the patient is medically fit to go.</v>

8
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.000
<v Ravi>Then one of us writes the discharge summary on the system.</v>

9
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:07.000
<v Ravi>Actually, when I can, I decide the evening before and write the summary then, so it's ready first thing.</v>

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
<v Interviewer>How hard is the summary to write, on a scale of one to five?</v>

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
<v Ravi>A three. It's not hard, it's just time I don't have.</v>

12
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:28.000
<v Helen>If their medicines have changed, the doctor writes the TTO, that's the to-take-out prescription.</v>

13
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
<v Helen>If nothing's changed, we just give them back their own medicines from the locker.</v>

14
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
<v Helen>The TTO goes to pharmacy and they dispense it. That can take two to four hours.</v>

15
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:53.000
<v Helen>Honestly, waiting for TTOs is the biggest hold-up. It happens most days, it drives me mad.</v>

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Helen>When it's late, I phone pharmacy to chase it.</v>

17
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:08.000
<v Helen>If they say it'll be ready within two hours, I carry on with everything else and check it when it comes up.</v>

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:18.000
<v Helen>If not, I walk down and collect it myself. I've done that loads of times, sometimes twice in a shift.</v>

19
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
<v Helen>Then I'm back to checking the medicines.</v>

20
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.000
<v Helen>When the medicines arrive, I check them against the discharge summary, every item.</v>

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.000
<v Interviewer>What happens next?</v>

22
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:43.000
<v Helen>Then I think about how they're getting home. If they need hospital transport, I book it on the transport portal.</v>

23
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
<v Helen>Transport confirm a pickup slot, usually by email.</v>

24
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.000
<v Helen>If family are collecting, I don't need to book anything.</v>

25
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:02.000
<v Helen>Then, do they need a care package at home? If yes, I refer them to the community care team.</v>

26
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:12.000
<v Helen>That can add a day, easily. Once they've agreed, I confirm the care start date with the family.</v>

27
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
<v Helen>If they don't need care, I skip all that.</v>

28
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:26.000
<v Helen>Then I take out their cannula. Um, I always do that before the teaching, so they're not distracted by it.</v>

29
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:34.000
<v Helen>Then I sit down with the patient and go through the discharge letter and every medicine.</v>

30
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.000
<v Ravi>I sometimes do the medicines talk myself if it's complicated, like a new anticoagulant.</v>

31
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:51.000
<v Helen>The patient asks questions and I check they've understood. I get them to tell it back to me.</v>

32
00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Helen>I give them a copy of the discharge letter. Um, and the GP gets it, er, electronically I think, the system sends it.</v>

33
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
<v Interviewer>And then?</v>

34
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.000
<v Helen>Then it depends. If transport's booked, the crew collect them from the ward.</v>

35
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.000
<v Helen>If not, family pick them up.</v>

36
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.000
<v Helen>Transport not turning up, that happens maybe once a week.</v>

37
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:32.000
<v Helen>When it does, I ring the transport desk. I've got all their numbers on a sticky note on the station, the portal's useless for that.</v>

38
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
<v Helen>Sometimes they can rebook for later that day and we carry on waiting for the crew.</v>

39
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.000
<v Helen>If they can't, I have to tell the patient they're staying another night.</v>

40
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:54.000
<v Helen>That happens a couple of times a month, and it breaks my heart every time.</v>

41
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
<v Helen>Once they've gone, I update the bed status on the system.</v>

42
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.000
<v Helen>And I tell the bed manager the bed's free, because they don't trust the system.</v>

43
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
<v Helen>And that's them discharged home.</v>

44
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.000
<v Interviewer>On a scale of one to five, how hard is the whole discharge for you?</v>

45
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
<v Helen>A four. On a bad day a five.</v>

46
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
<v Interviewer>And how long from medically fit to out the door?</v>

47
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
<v Helen>Four or five hours on a good day.</v>

48
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
<v Ravi>From my side it's more like half an hour of actual work, spread across the day.</v>
